        "id": "ch3_3_9-2",
        "text": "self.i_size = size\nself.i_seat = seat\ndef print_car_info (self):\nprint(f\"This car of color {self.i_color} with \\\nseats {self.i_seat} with engine of size \\\n{self.i_size}\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\ncar = Car (\"blue\", \"2.5L\", 5 )\ncar.print_vehicle_info()\ncar.print_engine_info()\ncar.print_car_info()\nIn this multiple inheritance example, we created two parent classes as a parent: Vehicle\nand Engine . The Vehicle parent class is the same as in the previous example. The\nEngine class has one attribute ( i_size ) and one method ( print_engine_info ).\nThe Car class is derived from both Vehicle and Engine and adds one additional\nattribute ( i_seats ) and one additional method ( print_car_info ). In the instance\nmethod, we can access instance attributes of both parent classes.\nIn the main program, we created an instance of the Car class. With this instance, we can\naccess the instance methods of parent classes as well as child classes. The console output of\nthe main program is shown here and is as expected:",
